## N2b exposure and corpus volume

`non-sequitur` clause N2b ("contradicted by the author's own text") exposes authors who have written more: Day's corpus is 39 Zenodo records, 154 kept blog posts and replies, and many of his arguments are multi-step; most critic claims are one-line identities from a single comment, post or talk. The clause is unchanged. Rates per 10,000 words:

| Measure | Day | Critics | Allies |
|---|---|---|---|
| error verdicts in numeric files / 10k words of quoted Statement text (scored text) | 22.7 (13 / 5715 words) | 10.0 (1 / 997 words) | 0.0 (0 / 267 words) |
| error verdicts (all files) / 10k words of harvested quote corpus (`quotes-day.md` 3018 words; `quotes-critics.md` 2944 words, critics and allies together) | 59.6 | 6.8 (critics + allies 2 errors) | |

The per-word rates adjust for quoted volume, not for the number of derivation steps per node, which was not computed. The harvested corpus is a sample chosen by the audit, not the authors' full output.

